window.docContents[210].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-induced-5-cycles":{"contents":"A copy of C[5] is only an injective homomorphism, so its image may have chords. In\na triangle-free graph it cannot: if c a and c b were adjacent for non-adjacent\na and b, then a and b would have a common neighbour k in C[5], and c a,\nc b and c k would form a triangle. So in a triangle-free graph every copy of\nC[5] is induced: it reflects adjacency as well as preserving it.\n\nlemma copy_cycleGraph_five_adj_iff {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W}\n    (h3 : H.CliqueFree 3) (c : Copy C[5] H) {a b : Fin 5} :\n    H.Adj (c a) (c b) ↔ C[5].Adj a b := by\n  classical\n  refine ⟨fun hab => ?_, c.toHom.map_adj⟩\n  by_contra hnab\n  obtain ⟨k, hak, hkb⟩ := (by decide : ∀ a b : Fin 5, ¬ C[5].Adj a b →\n    ∃ k, C[5].Adj a k ∧ C[5].Adj k b) a b hnab\n  exact h3 {c a, c b, c k}\n    (is3Clique_triple_iff.mpr ⟨hab, c.toHom.map_adj hak, (c.toHom.map_adj hkb).symm⟩)\n\n\nA copy that reflects adjacency is a graph embedding A ↪g B\n(Graph Embeddings), and Mathlib writes A ⊴ B (IsIndContained)\nfor the existence of one. So if every copy of A in B reflects adjacency, then the\ncopies of A in B and the embeddings A ↪g B are the same thing.Mathlib\nhas no induced analogue of labelledCopyCount: \"Count induced copies of a graph\ninside another\" is on the TODO list in Mathlib's Copy.lean.\n\n/-- If every copy of A in B reflects adjacency, then copies of A in B are the\nsame as graph embeddings A ↪g B. -/\ndef copyEquivEmbedding {W X : Type*} {A : SimpleGraph W} {B : SimpleGraph X}\n    (h : ∀ (f : Copy A B) {a b : W}, B.Adj (f a) (f b) → A.Adj a b) :\n    Copy A B ≃ (A ↪g B) where\n  toFun f := ⟨f.toEmbedding, ⟨h f, f.toHom.map_adj⟩⟩\n  invFun := Embedding.toCopy\n  left_inv _ := rfl\n  right_inv _ := rfl\n\n\nSince mμ[G] is triangle-free when G is\n(Triangle Free Mycielskians), the formula above also counts the\nembeddings of C[5] in mμ[G], i.e. its labelled induced 5-cycles.\n\ntheorem card_embedding_mycielskian_cycleGraph_five [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj]\n    (h3 : G.CliqueFree 3) (h5 : C[5].Free G) :\n    Nat.card (C[5] ↪g mμ[G]) =\n      5 * ∑ u, ∑ v ∈ G.neighborFinset u, G.degree u * G.degree v := by\n  rw [← Nat.card_congr (copyEquivEmbedding fun c _ _ =>\n      (copy_cycleGraph_five_adj_iff h3.mycielskian c).1), ← Fintype.card_eq_nat_card]\n  exact labelledCopyCount_mycielskian_cycleGraph_five h3 h5\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009Counting Copies*","header":"12.7.3. Induced 5-Cycles","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-induced-5-cycles"},"/Colorings/#section-greedy-colouring":{"contents":"Greedy colouring means constructing a colouring one vertex at a time, choosing the\nsmallest valid colour at each step, i.e. the smallest colour not used by any of the\nneighbours of v that we've already coloured. How many colours this uses depends\nnot on the degrees of the vertices but on the number of neighbours that precede\neach vertex in the order in which we colour them, so we start by defining this.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings","header":"7.8. Greedy Colouring*","id":"/Colorings/#section-greedy-colouring"}});